1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,033
Ridge adds a penalty on the size of the weights, with strength lambda. Positive lambda makes it well posed.

2
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:20,700
Setting the gradient to zero gives a closed form. It inverts X transpose X plus lambda times the identity.

3
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:28,933
A second form inverts an n by n matrix instead. Same estimator: use whichever matrix is smaller.

4
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:35,700
Take the SVD. Then ridge weights each direction by sigma over sigma squared plus lambda.

5
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:43,066
The pseudoinverse uses one over sigma. Ridge keeps the fraction sigma squared over sigma squared plus lambda.

6
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:49,333
Plot it. Large singular values are nearly untouched; small ones are strongly attenuated.

7
00:00:49,333 --> 00:00:56,933
At sigma equal to root lambda, exactly half is kept. A bigger lambda pushes that line right.

8
00:00:56,933 --> 00:01:03,500
Now the actual multiplier. Without ridge it is one over sigma, which explodes for weak directions.

9
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:11,300
With ridge it rises, peaks at one over two root lambda where sigma is root lambda, then falls.

10
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:18,900
Adding lambda times the identity shifts every eigenvalue up by lambda. The smallest gain the most, relatively.

11
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:27,100
The ravine from episode one has kappa 16. Ridge of 0.25 cuts it to 8.5, and ridge of 1 to 4.

12
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:34,366
The price is a different problem and a different answer. Conditioning and overfitting improve together.

13
00:01:34,366 --> 00:01:42,000
Data has strong energy along strong directions. Suppose the truth is small, and each direction carries noise.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:49,466
Without ridge we divide by sigma, so noise grows by one over sigma: thirty times more in the weakest direction.

15
00:01:49,466 --> 00:01:57,366
Ridge shrinks each direction by its own factor. Weak directions collapse toward zero; strong ones barely move.

16
00:01:57,366 --> 00:02:04,566
Averaged over noise, the error drops about twelvefold: calibrated distrust of directions that are mostly noise.

17
00:02:04,566 --> 00:02:12,433
Weak directions can hold signal, so suppressing them is a judgment. Overfitting trusts the data too much.

18
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:19,166
Gradient descent on the ridge objective shrinks the weights by a fixed factor, then takes the usual data step.

19
00:02:19,166 --> 00:02:27,433
That decay alone shrinks a weight by 0.9 each step. For plain gradient descent, ridge equals weight decay.

20
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:33,466
A Gaussian prior on the weights plus Gaussian noise makes the MAP estimate a ridge solution.

21
00:02:33,466 --> 00:02:41,133
One trap: minimizing the objective cannot pick lambda. It only grows with lambda, so lambda collapses to zero.

22
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:49,100
Lambda is a hyperparameter, chosen with held-out data. That is next, along with two other knobs.

